How the web client is delivered: assembled HTML, asset caching, the installed
app, and liveness across cache restores.

- Content-stamped assets: code-server keys static URLs by its repo commit,
  which this fork pins forever - the build takes COMPOSERY_STATIC_STAMP (the
  content hash build.sh computes) instead. The served HTML is
  Cache-Control: no-store because it embeds stamped URLs; service workers
  register with updateViaCache 'none'; each boot unregisters the webview
  service workers stranded under old stamps - storage a mobile WebView user has
  no way to clear.
- Overlay asset wiring: the workbench page links narrow.css / touch.css /
  fonts.css and loads shell.js from workbench-assets, carries the
  shared viewport contract (viewport-fit=cover,
  interactive-widget=resizes-content), and declares the favicon set: sized ICO
  fallback first, adaptive SVG last, with favicon.js swapping the
  scheme-pinned pair (Chromium rasterizes a favicon once per URL and never
  re-runs its embedded media query). favicon.js is the one the auth and error
  pages already load, from the same /_static path, not a second copy.
  Two neighbouring pages are deliberately left alone: workbench-dev.html, which
  is served only when the environment is not a built one and so never by our
  release image; and callback.html, which has no input to keep clear of a
  keyboard and no safe-area padding, so viewport-fit=cover there would only
  move its text under the notch that upstream's default keeps it out of.
- The installed app: standalone display, not fullscreen - the clock and
  battery stay visible, and window-controls-overlay is dropped with it because
  our titlebar reserves no titlebar-area-* room. Brand name (the
  apple-mobile-web-app-title iOS actually shows), description and colours are
  pinned to packages/shared by the "PWA install metadata" test. The manifest
  carries one colour for the Android splash (the dark tile of the launcher
  icon); theme-color metas then track the scheme per prefers-color-scheme.
- bfcache: a back/forward-cache restore hands back the DOM with every socket
  dead, so the workbench looks alive over a dead connection. no-store used to
  keep pages out of the bfcache; Chrome on Android now caches them anyway, so
  the page reloads itself on a persisted pageshow - `persisted` is set on a
  bfcache restore and nothing else.

- Webview content styles: narrow-width rules in the default stylesheet the
  webview pre page injects into extension content. No viewport contract here,
  and no safe-area padding: a webview is a nested browsing context, and
  Chromium honours neither in one. Measured on Android Chrome 134 - a subframe
  declaring `width=500, viewport-fit=cover` laid out at the parent's 411px,
  exactly like a subframe declaring no viewport at all, and read
  env(safe-area-inset-*) as 0 while the top frame read a real 24px bottom
  inset. So the meta, the safe-area padding, and the runtime repair that kept
  them on replaced documents were all no-ops and are gone.
  What is left is frame-independent: border-box + max-width so the 20px body
  padding cannot overflow a narrow viewport, and the text-size rules. Those
  styles live inside a template literal in the page's inline script, so
  the CSP hash beside them covers them; the patch-stack test recomputes it.

